I’ll first inspect the pattern, metadata, and the reusable refinement guidance in `learned/`. I’ll build a phase inventory from chemistry and peak evidence, then refine candidates in GSAS-II and verify that the exported CIFs, fractions, profile, and project agree exactly.

The chemistry points first to manganese tungstate, but I’m treating MnWO₄, unreacted MnO/WO₃, alternate Mn/W oxides, and polymorph/source-record variants as separate hypotheses. I’m now extracting the peak inventory and checking which indexing/search/refinement interfaces are available locally.

The observed peak family is already highly coherent with monoclinic wolframite MnWO₄: the low-angle 010/100 pair, the 011/110 pair, the split 11̄1/111 doublet, and many mid/high-angle families all recur at the expected spacings. The alternative Mn₃WO₆ and precursor/oxide patterns predict strong unique peaks that are absent. I’m moving to whole-pattern refinement now; residuals after a physically constrained one-phase baseline will decide whether any weak phase survives.

The first unconstrained profile trial exposed exactly the failure mode the added protocol warns about: with the correct reflection-mode displacement term released, the fit improved sharply, but the Gaussian \(U\) term ran strongly negative. I’m rejecting that nuisance model and rebuilding the common profile with \(U=0\), nonnegative \(W/X\), and the single specimen-shift correction retained; the cell has meanwhile moved to the known ambient wolframite metric, which is physically reassuring.

A physical one-parameter surface-roughness correction explains the angle-dependent intensity trend better than polarization: it lowers Rwp from 11.34% to 7.84%, whereas free polarization reaches 8.81%. Refining both roughness coefficients drove one negative and made them 99.7% correlated, so I rejected that over-parameterized form and retained only a positive roughness \(B\) term with \(A=0\). SH/L produced only a 0.003-point Rwp change and 96.6% correlation with displacement, so it is being pruned. Next I’m testing whether the remaining family-specific intensity residuals are resolved by physically sensible atomic coordinates rather than texture.

I found an important baseline issue in the GSAS-II defaults: the phase carried 1000 µstrain even though its strain refinement flag was off. That still contributes to the calculated width and explains why instrument \(U/W\) tried to compensate by going negative. Per the residual-led protocol, I’m rebuilding the profile with unused size/strain physically neutralized (large size, near-zero strain) before judging any broadening or texture term.

The rebuilt common profile is now physically stable: \(U=25.3\), \(W=10.6\), and \(X=6.95\), all positive, with Rwp 6.86%. Isotropic size is exactly degenerate with \(X\) (100% correlation), and an isotropic strain trial runs negative, so both are rejected; same-angle 030/022/220 families show comparable width residuals, giving no evidence for anisotropic broadening. I’m now re-challenging the phase watch list under this clean profile.

The clean residual does contain a real minor phase: three independent room-temperature WO₃ records refine to about 2.1 wt% and improve Rwp from 6.862% to 6.43–6.53%, while Mn₃WO₆ and both MnO records collapse to zero/insignificant scale. I’m refining the cells of the physically plausible monoclinic/triclinic WO₃ alternatives now, then I’ll rebuild the shared profile and repeat the impurity challenge so oxide false positives are not judged against the old one-phase residual.

After the two-phase profile rebuild, Mn₃WO₆ and alternate WO₃ polymorphs (orthorhombic/tetragonal) collapse, but three sub-percent candidates still pick up small positive scales. I’m checking those against their unique reflection families rather than accepting significance from scale alone; this is especially important for Mn₂O₃ and for a triclinic WO₃ record whose scale is strongly anticorrelated with the monoclinic WO₃ scale.

The weak manganese-oxide trials are not yet convincing: their gains come mainly from one or two low-intensity windows, while several of their strongest unique lines are absent or worsen the residual. I’m testing the more likely explanation for the WO₃ residual first—phase-specific broadening of the minor WO₃—because adding a second, highly correlated WO₃ polymorph can otherwise act as a peak-width surrogate.

The two-phase inventory is now clear enough to focus on the remaining ambiguity: whether the weak WO₃ peaks require a second polymorph or are explained by strain broadening in a single monoclinic phase. I’m testing replay stability and alternate WO₃ records, then I’ll re-challenge the trace manganese oxides under that final profile before exporting the exact project and deliverables.

The strain model is replay-stable and significant (about 6,600–7,700 µstrain, 7–8σ) for two independent monoclinic WO₃ records; the resulting WO₃ fraction is consistently about 3.0 wt%. The higher-quality neutron model remains slightly better, so I’m retaining it provisionally and now testing whether its broadening is isotropic enough and whether any manganese-oxide watch-list phase survives once that broadening is present.

The re-challenge removes Mn₃WO₆ and both MnO records (zero to <2σ) and leaves Mn₂O₃ at only ~1σ. Mn₃O₄ is the only trace candidate that strengthens when its cell is allowed to move (~1.1 wt%, plausible tetragonal cell), so I’m not discarding it on Rwp alone: I’m checking its strongest isolated reflection families against the residual, including predicted strong peaks that should be present but are not.

The Mn₃O₄ “signal” is not a coherent impurity pattern: its nominally unique 18.2° line is being used to fill the asymmetric shoulder of the intense MnWO₄ peak, while several strong Mn₃O₄ families near 36.6°, 56.1°, and 60.0° are absent or worsen the fit. I’m rejecting it as a profile surrogate, and I’m doing one final axial-asymmetry grid on the two-phase baseline so that this shoulder is handled—or explicitly left as model inadequacy—without inventing a phase.

The asymmetry grid found a materially better physical baseline: SH/L near 0.04–0.08 removes the low-angle shoulders across several MnWO₄ families. The highest trial drove the common W term slightly negative, so I’m locating the best replay-stable SH/L with U/W/X all nonnegative, then I’ll rebuild and re-challenge WO₃ strain and trace phases under that corrected profile.

The corrected baseline converges reproducibly at SH/L ≈ 0.071 with W fixed at the nonnegative boundary, U ≈ 64.5, and X ≈ 4.80. Under it, the WO₃ strain drops from an implausibly large ~0.66% to a modest, significant ~0.20%. I’m now neutralizing WO₃ broadening and rebuilding the common profile once more, then comparing size and strain one at a time as required.

With the corrected asymmetry, Mn₃WO₆ and MnO(a=4.41 Å) collapse, while Mn₂O₃ and the alternate MnO record remain sub-0.3 wt% residual-level fits without a clean family set. The only serious inventory question is now WO₃ polymorphism: adding triclinic WO₃ improves Rwp, but its peaks may be splitting the same broadened monoclinic families. I’m comparing a single triclinic model and mapping which improvements are genuinely distinct before deciding whether a second WO₃ CIF is defensible.

The phase inventory is resolving to two modeled crystalline phases: wolframite MnWO₄ and one low-symmetry WO₃ phase. The monoclinic/triclinic WO₃ distinction is not reliable enough to justify a second phase—the single-phase alternatives give nearly identical fits and the two-phase split is strongly anticorrelated with no unique dominant family. I’m retaining the slightly better monoclinic model, refining only the well-supported MnWO₄ coordinates, and then checking bond geometry before the final replay/export.

The final nuisance tests behave as expected for a dominant phase: MnWO₄ size is 99.97% correlated with the common X term and is rejected. A nominal MnWO₄ strain term lowers Rwp, but it trades directly against the common U profile and inflates the WO₃ strain, so I’m checking that covariance before pruning it. Texture gives nearly the same small improvement for several incompatible axes (ratios only ~0.98–1.02), which is axis-ambiguous rather than defensible preferred orientation.

The accepted model is now pruned: no MnWO₄ size/strain or texture, no extra oxide phases, and one isotropically strained WO₃ phase. The final MnWO₄ coordinates give normal octahedra (Mn–O 2.10–2.31 Å; W–O 1.77–2.14 Å). I’m performing the required save/reopen/full-range replay now, then all CIFs and the profile will be exported directly from that exact `final.gpx`.

All requested files now exist and were generated from the replayed `final.gpx`. I’m doing a final consistency audit: reopening the project, matching its fractions/statistics to `result.json`, numerically matching `fit.csv` to the stored profile, parsing both CIFs, and visually checking the low/mid/high-angle residuals.

Completed the crystalline-phase analysis:

- MnWO₄: 97.4537 wt%
- Monoclinic WO₃ model: 2.5463 wt%
- Rwp: 4.531%; GOF: 1.645

The exact WO₃ polymorph is not reliably distinguishable from triclinic WO₃; practical WO₃ uncertainty is approximately ±0.3 wt%.

